    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Darwin's five-year voyage on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a>, from 1831 to 1836, made his name as a geologist and gave him the fossils, finches, mockingbirds and tortoises that puzzled him into asking how species arise.</li><li>He devised the theory of natural selection in 1838 but published it only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace">Alfred Russel Wallace</a> sent him the same idea in 1858; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a> followed in 1859.</li><li>Evolution was widely accepted by the 1870s, but natural selection as its main mechanism won broad agreement only with the modern evolutionary synthesis of the 1930s to 1950s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Seeing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> orchid with a sixteen-inch nectary, Darwin predicted a moth with a proboscis long enough to pollinate it; explorers found it, Xanthopan, in 1903.</p></aside>
    </section>

